WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:11.610
Pancreatic cancer is the third leading cause
of cancer related death in the US and is predicted

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.780
to become the second leading cause of cancer
mortality by 2020.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.810
Despite recent advances, the 5-year survival
rate remains less than 7%.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:25.170
The majority of patients present with advanced
stage disease, and available treatments with

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:30.619
FOLFIRINOX or nab-paclitaxel plus gemcitabine
chemotherapy provide only modest survival

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:31.619
benefit.

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:36.150
In addition, patients who undergo attempts
at curative surgery plus adjuvant chemotherapy

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:42.470
still have a very poor 5-year survival rate
at roughly 15-20% with 80% of patients relapsing

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.040
after resection.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.660
These poor outcomes highlight the need for
novel development of biomarkers to predict

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.820
patient survival and treatment response with
potential linkage to different therapeutic

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.820
options.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:57.960
In general, the term “pancreatic cancer”
encompasses a mix of pathologies in which

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.740
the cells originate in the pancreas and are
thought to be epithelial in origin; however,

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.580
the question of the cellular origin of the
disease continues to be debated.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:12.110
Even with access to modern-day molecular profiling,
histology that distinguishes the exact pathologic

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:17.130
subtype of pancreatic lesions is still the
most clinically informative piece of data.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.110
Exocrine pancreatic cancers can have a gland-like
appearance and are the most common types of

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:26.460
pancreatic cancer; the majority are classified
as pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, commonly

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.260
referred to as PDAC or PDA.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.940
Other forms of exocrine cancers are rare but
are typically cystic or can be acinar cell

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.340
carcinomas.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.920
Endocrine cancers are a small subset of all
pancreatic cancers.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.160
Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors are classified
as functional hormone-producing or nonfunctional

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.850
non–hormone-producing.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.790
While histologic features are still used to
define and diagnose pancreatic cancer subtypes,

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:54.390
more recently, modern molecular techniques
such as next-generation DNA sequencing and

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.220
RNA sequencing have been used to further classify
pancreatic cancer subtypes.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:03.770
The hope is to discover better targets and
improve the therapeutic options.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.060
Several studies have examined gene expression
using microarrays or RNA sequencing and have

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.530
identified distinct RNA signatures of PDAC.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:17.219
Collisson et al originally described 3 PDAC
gene expression subtypes, including classical,

00:02:17.219 --> 00:02:20.219
quasi mesenchymal, and exocrine-like subtypes.

00:02:20.219 --> 00:02:25.050
Moffitt et al later used a “virtual microdissection”
approach on data from primary and metastatic

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:30.469
PDAC tumors to digitally separate tumor, stromal,
and normal cell gene expression.

00:02:30.469 --> 00:02:35.529
They identified 2 tumor-specific signatures,
including a classical subtype and a basal-like

00:02:35.529 --> 00:02:40.489
gene expression program that resembled the
basal subtype of breast and bladder cancers.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:45.319
Moreover, they also identified 2 stroma-specific
gene expression signatures and demonstrated

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:50.719
that combinations of the stroma and tumor-specific
subtypes represent distinct biology with different

00:02:50.719 --> 00:02:53.290
prognostic implications.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.790
The International Cancer Genome Consortium
subsequently defined 4 major gene expression

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.840
subtypes of PDAC, including a pancreatic progenitor
class that showed strong overlap with the

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.209
Collisson and Moffitt classical subtypes,
as well as a squamous classification that

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:10.390
closely resembles the Moffitt basal-like subtype.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.900
Additionally, the International Cancer Genome
Consortium study also described an aberrantly

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.950
differentiated endocrine exocrine subtype
and a novel immunogenic subtype that was associated

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.250
with evidence of a significant immune infiltration.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.329
These classifications are more similar than
different, and several common themes have

00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:28.340
emerged.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:33.129
The classical or pancreatic progenitor and
the basal-like or squamous neoplastic subtypes

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:38.200
have been validated across multiple studies
in primary and metastatic samples.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.969
Basal-like or squamous tumors harbor a significantly
worse prognosis than classical or progenitor

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:43.969
tumors.

00:03:43.969 --> 00:03:49.980
These basal-like or squamous tumors also display
more frequent TP53 mutations, a higher pathologic

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.799
grade, and a poorer response to modern chemotherapy
regimens.

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:58.430
Recently, the Cancer Genome Atlas project
provided further data suggesting that the

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:02.769
pancreatic progenitor or squamous subtype
was independent of purity.

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:07.279
In contrast, the ADEX or immunogenic subtype
have lower tumor purity.

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:12.319
The study also found that, among low-purity
tumors, a higher estimated leukocyte fraction

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:15.279
was associated with immunogenic samples.

00:04:15.279 --> 00:04:19.400
Considering only the high-purity samples in
the cohort, the squamous samples of Bailey

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:24.330
et al. showed significant overlap with the
basal-like samples defined by Moffitt et al.,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:29.070
while the Bailey et al. pancreatic progenitor
and Collisson et al. classical group largely

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.830
overlapped the classical samples defined by
Moffitt et al.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:36.879
These observations suggest that high-purity
tumors can be consistently classified into

00:04:36.879 --> 00:04:41.100
a basal-like/squamous group and a classical/progenitor
group.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.810
The strong association of immunogenic and
ADEX or exocrine-like subtypes with the low-purity

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:50.069
samples in the cohort suggests that these
subtypes may reflect gene expression from

00:04:50.069 --> 00:04:53.620
nonneoplastic cells.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:58.069
In summary, cross-comparison of the studies
does demonstrate that reproducible biologic

00:04:58.069 --> 00:05:00.289
subgroups are emerging in pancreatic cancer.

00:05:00.289 --> 00:05:01.289
While the nomenclature differs, there are
strong biological similarities between the

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:02.289
classical (Collisson and Moffitt), stable
(Waddell), and pancreatic progenitor (Bailey)

00:05:02.289 --> 00:05:03.289
subtypes.

00:05:03.289 --> 00:05:04.289
Likewise, the quasi-mesenchymal (Collisson),
unstable (Waddell), squamous (Bailey), and

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:05.289
basal-like (Moffitt) subtypes are biologically
similar.

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:06.289
Finally, the exocrine-like (Collisson) and
ADEX (Bailey) subtypes have overlapping characteristics.

00:05:06.289 --> 00:05:07.289
Nevertheless, a critical question is whether
these subgroups offer more prognostic or predictive

00:05:07.289 --> 00:05:10.389
clinical information about pancreatic cancer
than conventional pathology information.

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:15.659
Indeed, comparisons of the defined subtypes
suggest some prognostic relevance.

00:05:15.659 --> 00:05:20.010
In particular, the prognosis of the quasi-mesenchymal/squamous/basal-like
subtype is worse than that of the classical/pancreatic

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.770
progenitor subtype.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.240
Furthermore, there is the question of whether
patients with certain subtypes of pancreatic

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.340
cancer are more appropriate candidates for
novel therapies.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.650
For example, Bailey et al inferred that the
immunogenic subgroup will be more responsive

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.250
to immunotherapies.

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.490
Their reasoning is biologically sound, but
given the recent history of a lack of benefit

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:44.120
from immunotherapies in pancreatic cancer,
this hypothesis, too, must be thoroughly tested

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.979
in clinical trials, or at least by retrospective
analyses.

00:05:47.979 --> 00:05:51.919
Other relevant questions remain that may be
obstacles in moving this subtyping to the

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:57.770
clinic include, 1) Can subtypes change within
a tumor, i.e. how can we address intratumoral

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.229
heterogeneity?

00:05:59.229 --> 00:06:02.560
Can exposure to therapy change the subtype
of a tumor?

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.350
Can we design novel targeted drugs that can
benefit all subtypes?

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:11.860
Additionally, a byproduct of these deep genetic
analyses will inevitably be to identify distinct,

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:16.919
targetable molecular abnormalities; in order
to benefit an individual patient, however,

00:06:16.919 --> 00:08:52.949
these abnormalities will need to be identified
by real-time methods.

